# Data Layer Conventions

Schema definitions live in the PRD (`PRD-Watchly-2026-08-08.md`, §5) — this file is about
how to work with them, not a second copy of the DDL.

- Core tables: `users`, `friendships`, `rooms`, `room_participants`. Don't add new
  top-level tables for things that are really just fields on `rooms` (e.g. private-room
  password is a nullable `password_hash` column on `rooms`, not its own table).
- `users.id` matches Supabase Auth's `auth.users(id)` directly — no separate app-side
  user ID to keep in sync.
- Only durable data lives in Postgres: accounts, room configs, friendships. Fast-changing
  data does not — no `chat_messages` table (chat is ephemeral, broadcast-only via
  Socket.io) and no live playback position/participant-presence table. If a value changes
  more than once every few seconds, it belongs in the Socket.io server's memory, not here
  (see `realtime.mdc`).
- `room_participants` reflects current room membership as tracked by the Socket.io server;
  if it's persisted to Postgres at all, treat it as a snapshot for reference, not the
  live source of truth — the server's in-memory state wins.
- IDs are UUIDs generated at the DB level (`gen_random_uuid()`), not app-generated — keeps
  insertion logic simple and avoids collision handling. Room `uid` (the public join code)
  is a separate, shorter, unique string, generated server-side and checked against active
  rooms before assignment.
- Room passwords: store only `password_hash` (hashed server-side before insert), never
  plaintext. Validate on join server-side — never trust a client-side password check.
- Row Level Security (RLS): enable policies so users can only read/write friendship and
  room rows they're authorized to touch (e.g. a user can only see `friendships` rows
  where they're the requester or recipient; only a room's creator/admin can update it).
- Use the Supabase client directly for reads/writes — no ORM (Prisma, etc.) layered on
  top. Migrations go through the Supabase CLI, never a manual change against the dev or
  prod database.
